\pard\plain \sa180\sb180\ltrch\loch {Stepping through the doors of Liese Academy gave me the biggest d\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u233\'E9j\u224\'E0 vu I\u8217\'92ve ever had in my life. Not the mild kind, where you think, }{\f0\fs24\i1 oh, haven\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\af1\uc1\u8217\'92t I seen this corridor before?}{ No, this was the gut-punch kind, the kind where every brick, every echo off the stone walls whispered, }{\f0\fs24\i1 You\loch\af1\hich\af1\dbch\af1\uc1\u8217\'92ve been here before, Edward. And you left for a reason.}
\par\plain {The first thing I noticed was the cafeteria. It looked\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8230\'85 better. Cleaner, brighter. There were trays of food behind the glass, dishes I actually recognized. Real food\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 stuff I might\u8217\'92ve willingly eaten. Back in my day (gods, I sound old already), the old headmaster insisted on serving only \u8220\'93traditional\u8221\'94 Argantheian cuisine. Said it instilled culture and patriotism in the youth. Personally, I thought it just instilled stomach aches. If anything, those boiled roots and mystery meats made me less patriotic. If food could make you hate your country, Liese\u8217\'92s cafeteria had perfected the recipe.}
\par\plain {As I walked deeper into the halls, it was like every stone step remembered me, even if I didn\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92t want to remember it back. Mr. Freddy\u8217\'92s classroom was still tucked off the left wing, door scuffed from decades of bored students kicking at it. The old bulletin board was still sagging under the weight of announcements no one read. Even the corner where I used to sneak naps between lectures was still there, just as unassuming as ever.}
\par\plain {The bulletin board by the stairwell was still there, stuffed with notices and scribbled flyers. Even my old poster for the Alchemists Club was curling at the edges, yellowed and half torn.}
\par\plain {I stared at it longer than I meant to. I wondered if anyone was still carrying the torch, or if it had died the moment I left. Part of me hoped it was dead. Part of me hoped it wasn\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92t.}
\par\plain {We climbed the stairs together, the stone steps worn smooth by generations of boots, cloaks, and bad decisions. A few first-years darted past us, running up and down like startled rabbits. Not many of them, though. Which made sense\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 at this hour, they should\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92ve been locked away in their classes. Skipping, then. Bloody rascals. I couldn\u8217\'92t even be mad; I used to do the same thing back in first year, ducking out whenever the chance presented itself. Tradition, I suppose.}
\par\plain {The second floor opened into the main corridor of classrooms. Doors shut tight, muffled voices leaking through the limestone. A few sounded orderly\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 lectures, quills scratching paper. Others were chaos incarnate. Laughter, shouts, something clattering to the floor. Business as usual. If you didn\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92t break anything or leave the room, most teachers turned a blind eye.}
\par\plain {Mrs. Welton walked with purpose, her heels clicking against the stone. I trailed just half a step behind, trying not to feel like a kid being marched to his own sentencing.}
\par\plain {At the far end of the hall, looming like a bad omen, was the headmaster\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s office. The polished oak door with the heavy brass plate gleamed in the torchlight. My stomach tightened the way it always used to when I got dragged here. Fate had a funny way of dragging me back into old patterns.}
\par\plain {What did Torrie do?}
\par\plain 
\par\pard\plain \sa180\sb180\qc\ltrch\loch {-o-}
\par\pard\plain \sa180\sb180\ltrch\loch 
\par\plain {The headmaster\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s office felt less like an office and more like a tribunal. The long desk at the far end might as well have been a judge\u8217\'92s bench, and the air carried the heavy weight of judgment.}
\par\plain {On the right sat three adults, all wearing the same mean scowls as though they had been carved from the same bitter stone.}
\par\plain {On the left, Torrie. My kid sister looked small in her chair, twin tails bristling as though they had minds of their own. Mrs. Smith, her homeroom teacher, sat beside her like a lawyer defending a wayward client. I remembered her\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 stern face, stiff posture. She\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92d been Markus\u8217\'92s homeroom teacher back when I still roamed these halls. She hadn\u8217\'92t softened a bit.}
\par\plain {And at the center, presiding over it all, was Headmaster Aldwyn Greaves. Sharp-eyed, sharp-nosed, the kind of man who could turn silence into a weapon. His gaze landed on us the moment the door opened, and for a second it felt like the limestone walls themselves had leaned in to listen.}
\par\plain {The spell broke when Torrie whipped her head around, pigtails slicing the air like twin maces.}
\par\plain {\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Mom! Ed!\u8221\'94 she cried, bolting from her chair and throwing herself into Mrs. Welton\u8217\'92s arms.}
\par\plain {\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Hey, it\u8217\'92s alright, sweetie,\u8221\'94 Mom soothed her, patting her back with a tenderness I don\u8217\'92t think she\u8217\'92d ever extended to me when I\u8217\'92d been in that seat.}
\par\plain {\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Hey, you okay there, sport?\u8221\'94 I asked, trying to sound casual.}
\par\plain {\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93They started it, not me.\u8221\'94 Torrie pouted, clinging tighter.}
\par\plain {Mom swept into the room like she owned it. She greeted Headmaster Greaves first with a respectful nod, then Mrs. Smith, and then\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 most impressively\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 extended her hand to each of the three mothers glaring at us like they\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92d happily snuff out the Welton bloodline right then and there.}
\par\plain {To my surprise, they each took her hand. Reluctantly. Like they thought it might bite.}
\par\plain {\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93I understand your anger,\u8221\'94 Mom began, voice calm, firm, measured. She had that tone\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 the one that could soothe a crying child and silence a noisy tavern in equal measure. \u8220\'93Your sons\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 Oliver, Fred, and Jimmy\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 suffered injury at the hands of my daughter. I cannot imagine the fear you felt when they were carried to the infirmary.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93It was a fair duel!\u8221\'94 Torrie cut in from her seat, chin jutted out like a soldier holding a line.}
\par\plain {\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Shush!\u8221\'94 Mom hissed without even looking at her. One word, sharp as a lash, and Torrie clamped her mouth shut\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 though her glare could\u8217\'92ve melted steel.}
\par\plain {Turning back to the mothers, Mom folded her hands in her lap. \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93I apologize on behalf of my daughter, Torianna Welton. Whatever she believes, she is a child still. I will see to her discipline, and I will accept any retribution you feel is required to satisfy this matter.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Mom, isn\u8217\'92t that a bit too much?!\u8221\'94 Torrie\u8217\'92s voice cracked as she shot up in her chair.}
\par\plain {\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Sit.\u8221\'94 Mom\u8217\'92s reply was quiet, firm, and absolute. Torrie sank back, though her fists clenched white against her knees.}
\par\plain {Headmaster Greaves steepled his fingers, glancing first at Mom, then at the three mothers lined to his right. Their expressions were venomous\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 like they\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92d already won.}
\par\plain {\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93My Oliver has a fractured wrist and a wand snapped clean in half,\u8221\'94 one of the women barked. \u8220\'93Your daughter should pay for his medical expenses\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 and replace what she broke.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {Another chimed in before Mom could answer. \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Fred\u8217\'92s leg is bound up. That\u8217\'92ll be days in the infirmary. Who\u8217\'92s paying for the tonics, the charms, the healer\u8217\'92s time?\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93And Jimmy\u8217\'92s wand!\u8221\'94 the third spat, nearly trembling with indignation. \u8220\'93A carved ash piece, imported from Edenfield! Do you know what that cost us? Do you?\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {Greaves raised a hand, but it wasn\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92t to silence them. It was just to pace the flood. \u8220\'93Ladies, one at a time. But the point stands.\u8221\'94 He turned his gaze to Mom.}
\par\plain {Mom inclined her head with all the grace of a queen stepping off her throne. \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Whatever it costs. Every crown, every shard of wood. My daughter\u8217\'92s folly will not be yours to shoulder.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {Torrie shot upright again. \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Mom! You can\u8217\'92t do this!\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {Her voice rang louder than it should\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92ve, echoing against the limestone walls. My gut twisted, but I knew what was coming.}
\par\plain {\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93What you did was wrong, Torianna,\u8221\'94 Mom snapped, steel slipping into her tone for the first time that day. \u8220\'93And when we do wrong, we right it.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93But they\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 !\u8221\'94 Torrie\u8217\'92s eyes brimmed hot as she half-stood, pointing across the room. \u8220\'93They called you the W word, Mom!\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {Mom\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s expression had changed. When we first entered, she carried herself with warmth, with that careful balance of charm and authority she always wielded like a shield. But now\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 now her face softened into something heavier, like pity. Not for the other mothers, not even for the boys in the infirmary. For Torrie. As though she had seen this play out a dozen times before and already knew the ending.}
\par\plain {\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Edward,\u8221\'94 Mom said, her voice quiet but leaving no room for refusal. \u8220\'93Do you mind going for a walk with Torrie for just a moment? I will fetch you when things are done.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Mom!\u8221\'94 Torrie shot back, cheeks red, eyes glassy with anger and humiliation.}
\par\plain {But I understood. I always did.}
\par\plain {\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93You heard Mom, Torrie,\u8221\'94 I said, rising from my chair, my voice pitched in that casual older-brother drawl I used whenever I needed her to move. \u8220\'93Come on. Show me around to the new stuff, eh? I\u8217\'92m curious to see what\u8217\'92s changed.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93I don\u8217\'92t wanna.\u8221\'94 She folded her arms across her chest, lips pulled into a pout.}
\par\plain {I leaned closer, lowering my voice just enough for her to hear. \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93That\u8217\'92s not a suggestion.\u8221\'94 I held her gaze until she broke it. \u8220\'93Come on. Let the adults do their things.\u8221\'94\line }}